# Ordinary text is normalized across platforms. More specific rules below win.
* text=auto eol=lf
execution/**/verifier/**/verifier -filter -ident -working-tree-encoding -merge -text

# Canonical records are raw Git blobs. No checkout filter, keyword expansion,
# working-tree encoding, or semantic merge driver may transform them.
#
# records/ was on `diff text eol=lf` while all three sibling Frontiers had it
# on `-text`, and `-text` is the correct one: these blobs are named by their
# own sha256, and end-of-line normalization is a byte rewrite, so a record it
# touches no longer hashes to its own filename. Nothing under records/ here
# holds a CR today, which is exactly why this was invisible; the sibling
# formal-conjectures Frontier already stores a gzip stream at
# records/artifacts/sha256/3a1f083e…b26c carrying six CRLF pairs, and this
# repository accepts the same kind of Artifact. The lost `diff` is the price:
# these are content-addressed blobs, and a textual diff of one is not what a
# reviewer reads anyway.
.vela/** -filter -ident -working-tree-encoding -merge -text
records/** -filter -ident -working-tree-encoding -merge -text
sources/** -filter -ident -working-tree-encoding -merge -text
artifacts/** -filter -ident -working-tree-encoding -merge -text
review/** -filter -ident -working-tree-encoding -merge -text
frontier.toml -filter -ident -working-tree-encoding -merge diff text eol=lf

# Witnesses are small reviewed JSON, and 21 of the 38 tracked here are byte
# identical to a canonical Artifact under records/artifacts/sha256/: the
# witness is the preimage of its own digest. So they get the same `-text` rule
# for the same reason, not the softer one they used to carry. The global
# `* text=auto eol=lf` above is what makes an explicit rule necessary.
witnesses/** -filter -ident -working-tree-encoding -merge -text
